.\" Keep this page current: it describes the demo demos/y2k.bas (an executable made with BUILD, installed by "make install").
.TH ENGINE1999-Y2K 6 "November 2026" "Engine 1999 @VERSION@" "Engine1999 Manual"
.SH NAME
engine1999-y2k \- Y2K Countdown, a silly demo in the style of the late 1990s
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B engine1999-y2k
.RI [ part ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A demo of about two minutes, in nine parts, to a dance tune that the program makes by itself (bass, lead, arpeggio, pads and drums from the synthesizer's instruments): the intro with falling letters, copper bars, a starfield with a flying saucer, a plasma with bobs, vector balls that morph from a cube into a sphere, a torus and a double helix above a grid floor, a spiral tunnel, a sine scroller with greetings, a landscape with three layers of hills, tiles and a bug, and at last the millennium bug: a countdown, a crash, a boot screen and fireworks over a skyline.
.PP
It shows what the engine can do: palette cycling (plasma, tunnel), two graphics layers that take turns (no flicker), sprites as balls and as letters, tiles scrolled with wrap-around, the text screen as a color gradient, translucent flashes, sound effects and eight music channels with the synthesizer's instruments. All motion follows the clock, so the demo runs at the same speed on a fast and on a slow machine. The program is the demo \fIy2k.bas\fR of Engine1999, made into an executable with
.B BUILD
(window kind: it needs the SDL2 library, nothing else). The window can be switched between the full screen and a window with F11 or ALT+RETURN.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I part
a number from 0 to 8: start the demo at that part (0 intro, 1 copper bars, 2 starfield, 3 plasma, 4 vector balls, 5 tunnel, 6 scroller, 7 landscape, 8 the finale), the music starting there too.
.SH CONTROLS
.TP
.B "Q, ESC"
end the demo.
.TP
.B "left / right mouse button"
in the scroller part (greetings): hold the left button to slow the text down, the right button to speed it up.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR engine1999 (1),
.BR engine1999\-basic (7)
